-- #1110 PR C corpus — DECLARE AND RE-EXPORT THE SAME NAME.  This module is the
-- witness for #1245 (FIXED), in BOTH namespaces.
--
-- `mid` re-exports `deep`'s `Relay` (interface) and `Baton` (type) AND declares a
-- `Relay` and a `Baton` of its own.  What a consumer writing `import mid.{Relay,
-- hop}` actually binds is **mid's own** interface — verified by method identity on
-- the binary this fixture was written against, not inferred:
--
--     import mid.{Relay, hop} ; impl Relay Int where hop n = n   -> exit 0
--     import mid.{Relay, via} ; impl Relay Int where via n = n
--       -> "Method 'via' is not part of interface 'Relay'", exit 1
--
-- The type namespace binds mid's own the same way, verified the same way (two
-- modules declaring `data Bar`, the second re-exporting the first's: the consumer
-- can name the SECOND's constructor, and the first's is *"Unbound variable …
-- Did you mean …"*).
--
-- 🚨 SO THE GOLDEN MUST SAY `mod:mid` FOR BOTH — `iface:Relay 1 mod:mid` and
-- `Baton 1 mod:mid`.  Until #1245 it said `mod:deep`: `known["mid"]` is built by
-- `typeOriginExports`, which concatenated own-declarations FIRST and re-exports
-- LAST while every consumer folds it LAST-WRITE-WINS (`omFromPairs`,
-- `support/ordmap.mdk`), so the re-exported source won and `bindTypeOrigin` handed
-- the consumer an id for a module that did not declare what it bound.  The fix
-- reordered that concatenation to re-exports first, own last, agreeing with
-- `tyOriginScope` and with what resolve binds.
--
-- ⚠️ MEASURED, by rebuilding at the pre-fix ordering with this corpus in place:
-- the `Baton` spread line read `Baton 2 mod:deep|mod:mid`, not `1 mod:deep`.
-- `mid`'s OWN occurrence (`carry : Baton -> Int`, below) is stamped from `mid`'s
-- own `tyOriginScope`, where the own-declaration layer wins, so `mid` said
-- `mod:mid` while its consumer said `mod:deep` — ONE binding, TWO identities, in
-- one program.  That is the sharpest form of the defect and it is why this
-- fixture writes an occurrence on BOTH ends of the chain rather than only in
-- `main_ifaces`; `iface:Relay` cannot show it, because no module here writes a
-- `Relay` occurrence except the consumer.
--
-- ⚠️ BOTH NAMESPACES ARE PINNED HERE DELIBERATELY.  The two share one
-- concatenation, so a regression would move both rows — but "they share a
-- function" is a claim about the source, and this gate is the only place in the
-- tree where an origin is OBSERVABLE at all.  Do not drop either row.

export import deep.{Relay, Baton}

export interface Relay a where
  hop : a -> Int

export data Baton = MidBaton

-- Gives `main_ifaces` a way to write a `Baton` OCCURRENCE without exporting any
-- constructor: `Baton` is abstract on both ends, so the corpus pins the type-side
-- origin without also dragging in the cross-module constructor-merge shape.
export carry : Baton -> Int
carry MidBaton = 1
